h (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). So those are diagonal lines parallel to x+y=0.\n\nThus many points are collinear on these three families: vertical, horizontal, diagonal (sum constant). Those are non-sunny lines. Sunny lines are any other lines.\n\nNow, we need to cover all points with 4 lines. If we use only non-sunny lines, we could use vertical lines x=1,2,3,4 (k=0). That's 4 lines, works. If we want some sunny lines, we need to replace some of those with sunny lines. For k=1, we could replace one vertical line with a sunny line that covers the points of that column. For column 4, only point (4,1). So we can replace x=4 with a sunny line passing through (4,1) and maybe some other points. So k=1 works.\n\nFor k=2, we need to replace two vertical lines. The columns with few points are column 4 (1 point) and column 3 (2 points). So we need to cover points (3,1),(3,2),(4,1) with two sunny lines. Is that possible? Let's try to find two sunny lines covering those three points. As we saw earlier, we can use line through (3,2) and (2,1) (slope (1-2)/(2-3) = -1/(-1) = 1) which is sunny. That covers (3,2) and (2,1). Then we need to cover (3,1) and (4,1). Could we use a line through (3,1) and (4,1)? That's horizontal, not sunny. So we need a sunny line covering (3,1) and (4,1) maybe with another point. For example, line through (3,1) and (2,2) has slope (2-1)/(2-3) = 1/(-1) = -1, which is diagonal (parallel to x+y=0), not sunny. Line through (3,1) and (1,3) has slope (3-1)/(1-3) = 2/(-2) = -1, again diagonal. Line through (3,1) and (1,2) has slope (2-1)/(1-3) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, which is sunny. So we can have a sunny line L2: through (3,1) and (1,2). That covers (3,1) and (1,2) (already covered by vertical line x=1). Then we still need to cover (4,1). Could we use the same line L2 to also cover (4,1)? No, because line through (3,1) and (1,2) does not pass through (4,1). So we need a third line to cover (4,1). But we only have 2 sunny lines. So we need to cover (4,1) with one of the 